Thus hide should call button.focus().

Why didn't focus move? Possibly because the button is not focusable at that moment? Maybe the button lost focus due to something else? Or maybe the hide method is called but the focus restoration occurs before the dialog is hidden? Actually they set aria-hidden true then focus. Should work.

But observed after Escape: dialog still has aria-hidden="true" (so hidden) but focus remained on dialog. Means focus() call didn't shift focus.

Potential reason: the button might be disabled or not focusable due to some attribute? Not in HTML.

Maybe the issue is that the dialog still has tabindex="-1" and is still focusable (though hidden). When hide runs, focus is set to button, but then maybe the maintainFocus listener (still active?) moves focus back? They removed focus event listener after hiding: document.body.removeEventListener('focus', this.maintainFocus, true). That occurs after focus() call. So after focus() call, the maintainFocus listener is still active until that line runs. Could the focus() call trigger a focus event, which maintainFocus listener then moves focus back to dialog? Let's see: maintainFocus is bound to body capture phase true. When focus shifts to button, a focus event fires on button, bubbles up to body, captured, maintainFocus runs. It checks if active element is within a dialog with aria-modal true or has ignore attribute. At that moment, the dialog still has aria-modal="true" (unchanged) and is still in DOM, but aria-hidden="true". However maintainFocus does not check aria-hidden; it only checks if closest('[aria-modal="true"], [data-a11y-dialog-ignore-focus-trap]', target). Since dialog still has aria-modal="true", and the target (button) is not inside that dialog, closest will return null? Actually closest looks up from target (button) for ancestors matching selector. The button is not inside dialog, so closest returns null. Then condition !closest(...) => true, so focus(this.$el) is called, moving focus back to dialog. That would cause focus to return to dialog after we attempted to focus button. Then after that, the maintainFocus listener is removed (by the line after). So final focus ends up on dialog.

Thus the bug: maintainFocus listener should be removed before attempting to restore focus, or the maintainFocus should check also if dialog is hidden (aria-hidden true) to not trap focus when dialog is hidden.

The typical pattern in a11y-dialog is to remove the focus listener before restoring focus, to avoid this race. Indeed many implementations do: hide -> remove listeners -> restore focus. Let's verify: In the provided code, they set aria-hidden true, then focus, then remove listeners. That's wrong order.

Thus fix: In hide method, move the removal of listeners before calling this.previouslyFocused?.focus?.().

Alternatively, adjust maintainFocus to check aria-hidden, but simpler to reorder.

